{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I know you're not feeling well. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital tonight?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been throwing up for four days, and my stomach hurts so bad. It’s mostly in my upper belly, but it’s everywhere now. I had a fever too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you mentioned vomiting—was it yellow or did you see any blood?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yellow, no blood. I couldn’t keep anything down. I stopped my insulin pump two days ago because I wasn’t eating."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:41:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. I want to clarify—right now, are you using an insulin pump at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I should be. I keep asking for one, but my endocrinologist won’t give it to me. I don’t like giving myself shots all the time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. But right now, you’re on Lantus at night and Humalog with meals, correct? That’s what we have on record."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:46:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, but I wasn’t using it when I got sick. I just couldn’t."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. Your blood sugar was very high when you arrived—736. We gave you IV insulin, and it’s already coming down. You were in mild diabetic ketoacidosis, but the anion gap has closed, which is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:50:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I’m not in DKA anymore?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2158-01-22 03:51:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, not anymore. The treatment worked quickly. We think your vomiting and not taking insulin led to the high sugars. The good news is, your symptoms are improving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2158-01-22 06:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. Your latest blood sugar is 350, which is still high but much better. You’re tolerating ice cream and crackers, which is a good sign your stomach is settling."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2158-01-22 06:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel less nauseous. Can I go home? I just want to be in my own bed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2158-01-22 06:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I hear you. We’re considering discharge. But first, we need to make sure you’re safe to manage your insulin at home. You’ll need to follow up with your primary doctor in two days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2158-01-22 06:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. I know how to dose my Humalog. I use the sliding scale I’ve used before, starting at 150."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2158-01-22 06:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. We’ll keep your insulin the same—60 units of Lantus at bedtime and your usual Humalog sliding scale. No changes. We also continued your hydromorphone for pain and omeprazole for your stomach."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2158-01-22 12:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve been in touch with your PCP. They agree you can go home today as long as you keep that follow-up appointment. How are you feeling now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2158-01-22 12:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. I’m sorry I was upset earlier. I just hate being here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2158-01-22 12:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s okay. We understand this is hard. Just remember—your port in your chest wasn’t working well for blood draws, so we de-accessed it. You’ll need a follow-up with the IV team in a month to check it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2158-01-22 12:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. I’ll make sure to go."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2158-01-22 12:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your discharge instructions say no medication changes. Keep taking everything as before. And please don’t stop your insulin, even if you’re not eating well. Maybe just adjust the dose, not skip it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2158-01-22 12:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll try. I just get so sick sometimes, I don’t know what to do."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2158-01-22 12:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s why your PCP is so important. They know you well. And if you’re vomiting again, come back sooner rather than waiting four days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2158-01-22 12:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you for not making me stay longer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2158-01-22 12:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’re discharging you home. A nurse will go over your meds one more time before you leave."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2158-01-22 13:15:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One thing—my asthma’s been okay, but I’ll keep using my inhalers like normal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2158-01-22 13:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Fluticasone-salmeterol twice a day, and Combivent if you’re short of breath. You’re doing the right things—just keep it up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2158-01-22 15:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "All set. You’re cleared to go home. Take care, and see your doctor in two days."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2158-01-22 03:34:00",
    "admission_end": "2158-01-22 15:15:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with a four-day history of nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain, during which she stopped her insulin therapy, leading to severe hyperglycemia and mild diabetic ketoacidosis. IV insulin rapidly corrected her anion gap, and her symptoms improved with supportive care. She was restarted on her home regimen of 60 units of Lantus at bedtime and her usual Humalog sliding scale, with continuation of oral hydromorphone for pain and omeprazole for gastric protection. Her condition stabilized within hours, and after coordination with her primary care provider, she was discharged home with instructions to follow up in two days. No medication changes were made, and she demonstrated understanding of her insulin management. The port in her chest was de-accessed due to poor function and will require outpatient evaluation.",
    "problems": [
      "Nausea with vomiting",
      "Diabetes with ketoacidosis, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Long-term (current) use of insulin",
      "Abdominal pain, unspecified site",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
